WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:04.460
Speaker 1: Today we will be speaking with
Dr. John Abramson, who's been on the faculty

00:00:04.460 --> 00:00:09.139
of Harvard Medical School for 25 years and
is author of the new book Sickening How Big

00:00:09.139 --> 00:00:14.299
Pharma Broke American Health Care and How
We Can Repair It. A really great to have you

00:00:14.299 --> 00:00:18.200
on today and I appreciate your time.
Speaker 6: David, it's a pleasure to be with

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:20.360
you. Thanks.
Speaker 1: So, you know, when we talk about

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:25.370
the modern state of American health care,
there's many cooks in the kitchen, so to speak.

00:00:25.370 --> 00:00:31.140
And we can talk about insurance companies.
We can talk about big pharma, we can talk

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:36.720
about our political system. And there's all
of these different factors. In your book,

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:42.260
you talk specifically about big pharma. Talk
to us about why, as a doctor, that's where

00:00:42.260 --> 00:00:48.750
you decided to focus in this book.
Speaker 6: Right. So I think the biggest American

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:54.370
medicine has enormous problems are costs are
way off the charts. The health of Americans

00:00:54.370 --> 00:01:00.370
is so inferior to the other wealthy countries
that about 480,000 Americans die in excess

00:01:00.370 --> 00:01:06.540
each year. There's trouble all over the place.
I think the biggest cause of this trouble

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:12.400
that is unrecognized is the extent to which
the pharmaceutical and device industries control

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:19.470
the knowledge that doctors rely upon to formulate
their ideas about the best way to treat their

00:01:19.470 --> 00:01:26.030
patients.
Speaker 1: So explain how that leads to particular

00:01:26.030 --> 00:01:30.970
outcomes and disease management, which is
often what it is that that doctors are doing

00:01:30.970 --> 00:01:39.100
quite, quite frankly. How what is that relationship?
Speaker 6: So let's look at the cancer moonshot

00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:42.020
issue, which is in the news now with President
Biden.

00:01:42.020 --> 00:01:46.540
Speaker 1: Yeah.
Speaker 6: The anti-cancer drugs dominate

00:01:46.540 --> 00:01:55.220
clinical research. They're very expensive.
We could cut cancer deaths in half in the

00:01:55.220 --> 00:02:04.829
United States by attending to preventive therapies.
Preventive interventions stop smoking, obesity,

00:02:04.829 --> 00:02:11.350
environmental risks. So what we're doing because
the money's to be made in developing new drugs

00:02:11.350 --> 00:02:18.811
and marketing them. What we do is create a
body of of of so-called knowledge. That's

00:02:18.811 --> 00:02:24.970
the result of research that's commercially
sponsored. That's about drugs, drug therapies

00:02:24.970 --> 00:02:31.650
for cancer. And the preventive side gets virtually
no attention because there's no money to be

00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:39.430
made in it. What we've got is a a market failure
where the money is invested in high cost therapies,

00:02:39.430 --> 00:02:44.940
whereas the benefit could be reaped much more
quickly, less expensive, more effectively

00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:49.959
by attending to preventive approaches.
Speaker 1: So at the primary care level, often

00:02:49.959 --> 00:02:58.150
an idea is floated that you could compensate
doctors not based on the number of things

00:02:58.150 --> 00:03:04.650
that are done, for lack of a better term,
but instead for the sort of successful management

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:09.700
of a patient and actually keeping them healthy
rather than sick. And of course, the devil

00:03:09.700 --> 00:03:14.090
would be in the details in terms of exactly
how you do it and how you measure and how

00:03:14.090 --> 00:03:18.650
you compensate. But that's an idea that's
mentioned to put the focus back on prevention

00:03:18.650 --> 00:03:24.290
rather than treatment. Does that not if you
could do that, does that not even really do

00:03:24.290 --> 00:03:27.739
enough? Because what you're describing is
so widespread and established?

00:03:27.739 --> 00:03:34.670
Speaker 6: Yeah, it doesn't do enough. It's
a good idea. I think that paying primary care

00:03:34.670 --> 00:03:41.330
docs to take care of people instead of performing
units of work would be a good idea. And I

00:03:41.330 --> 00:03:46.450
think trying to minimize the number of procedures
that are done by changing the payment for

00:03:46.450 --> 00:03:51.150
those procedures is a good idea. And that
would be more effective with specialists than

00:03:51.150 --> 00:03:55.299
it would be with primary care docs. So you
could capitated specialists for a geographic

00:03:55.299 --> 00:04:01.730
area and they would make so much money on
salary. And if they did extra, say, cardiac

00:04:01.730 --> 00:04:07.370
surgeries, there wouldn't make money. You
know, if they did extra breast biopsies, that

00:04:07.370 --> 00:04:12.930
wouldn't make money. So that that's one way
to approach it. But the really the core lesion

00:04:12.930 --> 00:04:22.280
is that the actual knowledge that doctors
are trained to trust is invested in because

00:04:22.280 --> 00:04:27.539
it's going to maximize financial return to
the drug companies. It's that knowledge that's

00:04:27.539 --> 00:04:34.419
the problem. And the idea that those that
that the research projects get turned into

00:04:34.419 --> 00:04:39.930
articles in medical journals that are mostly
written by the drug companies that are peer

00:04:39.930 --> 00:04:44.360
reviewed. But virtually nobody in health care
understands that peer reviewers don't have

00:04:44.360 --> 00:04:50.969
access to the data and the guidelines that
are written that set the standards for good

00:04:50.969 --> 00:04:58.629
community practice and can be produced as
evidence in malpractise cases if you don't

00:04:58.629 --> 00:05:03.801
follow them. Those guidelines. Writers don't
have access to the clinical trial data. And

00:05:03.801 --> 00:05:09.240
having spent ten years in litigation, I know
that what's in that clinical trial data, the

00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:14.599
real data is often very different from the
data that ends up in medical journals. So

00:05:14.599 --> 00:05:19.860
what we've got is doctors who are compelled
to practice so-called evidence based medicine,

00:05:19.860 --> 00:05:25.509
which is means the bottom two lines of the
abstract in the medical journal. And that's

00:05:25.509 --> 00:05:31.000
what they said, without understanding that
the whole research enterprises is biased towards

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:36.559
making money, that the results aren't peer
reviewed, that the guidelines aren't peer

00:05:36.559 --> 00:05:41.430
reviewed. And what the doctors have been trained
to trust is the information that's going to

00:05:41.430 --> 00:05:44.749
best help them take care of their patients
is not trustworthy.

00:05:44.749 --> 00:05:49.720
Speaker 1: There's this example that I haven't
read about, admittedly for a little bit, so

00:05:49.720 --> 00:05:54.149
I hope I get the details mostly right, and
I think maybe it's related to exactly the

00:05:54.149 --> 00:05:58.840
mechanism you're talking about, which is when
it came to cholesterol, there was a point

00:05:58.840 --> 00:06:02.289
in time and again, I don't know if it was
a couple of years ago or more like a decade

00:06:02.289 --> 00:06:08.969
or 12 years ago, where the sort of line at
which point one might consider recommending

00:06:08.969 --> 00:06:14.639
a statin to a patient was lowered. So basically
just the threshold was changed. Nothing really

00:06:14.639 --> 00:06:19.909
changed about one's likelihood of a cardiac
event. Nothing. Nothing really changed other

00:06:19.909 --> 00:06:24.749
than instead of saying, I don't know if it
was from 210 to 200 or 222, just a change

00:06:24.749 --> 00:06:30.340
was made. And it was. Now, if the patient
is above this level, consider a statin. I

00:06:30.340 --> 00:06:34.369
don't know the origins of that change. I don't
know if that change was based in what we might

00:06:34.369 --> 00:06:39.699
call good, good science or as you talk about
kind of profit driven science or something

00:06:39.699 --> 00:06:44.520
else. But would that be the type of thing
that is influencing doctors to make certain

00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:48.379
decisions that can be very profitable?
Speaker 6: That's exactly right. And there

00:06:48.379 --> 00:06:53.069
are two aspects to that. One is there's never
been a study that compared statin therapy

00:06:53.069 --> 00:06:57.450
to lifestyle intervention. So the drug companies
don't want to do that study because they can

00:06:57.450 --> 00:07:02.620
only lose because they have all the statin
business. The other part is that I was the

00:07:02.620 --> 00:07:08.319
lead author of an article in the British Medical
Journal in 2013, which was exactly the time

00:07:08.319 --> 00:07:13.569
that you're talking about when the standards
were changed. Yeah. And the numbers for cholesterol,

00:07:13.569 --> 00:07:18.749
the study results are not transparent. Docs
don't have them, guideline writers don't have

00:07:18.749 --> 00:07:24.529
them. But there's an organization in Oxford
called the Cholesterol Treatment Trials Collaboration

00:07:24.529 --> 00:07:30.939
that has the data. They made a fashion bargain
with the drug companies that they would receive

00:07:30.939 --> 00:07:38.309
their patient level data and do meta analysis
on that data. And by combining the statistical

00:07:38.309 --> 00:07:45.879
power of all the different statin drugs effects
in clinical trials, they would be able to

00:07:45.879 --> 00:07:54.599
show that statins worked for more people.
Excuse me. So the city receives the primary

00:07:54.599 --> 00:08:00.050
data, but part of their Faustian bargain was
they wouldn't release the primary data. So

00:08:00.050 --> 00:08:05.779
they control what doctors think they know
about these things. So I wrote I was the lead

00:08:05.779 --> 00:08:12.710
author of this article in BMJ and we recalculated
the CTS numbers that they had published in

00:08:12.710 --> 00:08:18.249
The Lancet that justified lowering the threshold
for treating people with cholesterol lowering

00:08:18.249 --> 00:08:22.959
medication. And we showed that for people
at low risk who don't have heart disease,

00:08:22.959 --> 00:08:29.189
who have less than 20%, ten year risk, that
there was not a mortality benefit, that that

00:08:29.189 --> 00:08:34.360
their data didn't show a mortality benefit
and that you had to treat between 100 and

00:08:34.360 --> 00:08:41.060
140 people for five years with a statin to
prevent one nonfatal heart attack or stroke.

00:08:41.060 --> 00:08:46.050
So we wrote that and unfortunately, we made
a little mistake in interpreting the frequency

00:08:46.050 --> 00:08:51.180
of side effects in a complex study, but it
had nothing to do with our conclusion and

00:08:51.180 --> 00:08:57.230
the head of the city went absolutely ballistic.
Charged into the BMJ editor's office and demanded

00:08:57.230 --> 00:09:02.620
retraction of our article, which yeah, it
was an international kerfuffle. It didn't

00:09:02.620 --> 00:09:06.721
get much coverage here in the United States,
but it sure got coverage in Europe and it

00:09:06.721 --> 00:09:14.980
was pretty stressful. The editor appointed
a six person panel to independently evaluate

00:09:14.980 --> 00:09:19.850
the demand for retraction. The panel voted
unanimously that there was nothing about our

00:09:19.850 --> 00:09:27.560
article that required a deserved or merited
retraction. And on we went. But the CTT being

00:09:27.560 --> 00:09:34.710
about a million times more powerful than me
and my three coauthors had dominated the press.

00:09:34.710 --> 00:09:42.579
The headlines so long about this attack on
statins that we couldn't get our message across

00:09:42.579 --> 00:09:49.480
to the public, which is, hey, if you are a
low risk person, you may want to take statins,

00:09:49.480 --> 00:09:53.670
but you got to understand it's not going to
improve your longevity and you have to treat

00:09:53.670 --> 00:09:59.480
100, 240 people for five years in order for
one to avoid one nonfatal cardiovascular event.

00:09:59.480 --> 00:10:03.459
And that's your decision? It's not you know,
I'm not saying yes or no. I'm saying doctors

00:10:03.459 --> 00:10:08.430
should tell patients that that's what the
situation is and make let the patients make

00:10:08.430 --> 00:10:12.730
a decision based on their own values.
Speaker 1: Right. To talk about something

00:10:12.730 --> 00:10:18.470
a little more, maybe superficial as it might
be within the big pharma space. No, my dad's

00:10:18.470 --> 00:10:23.259
a psychiatrist, and he will often talk to
me about pharmaceutical reps, come to visit

00:10:23.259 --> 00:10:27.970
and they bring little footballs and they bring
pens and they bring, you know, notepads and

00:10:27.970 --> 00:10:32.310
all of this sort of thing. And they drop off
literature and sometimes there's invitations

00:10:32.310 --> 00:10:36.890
to lunch and sometimes there's other things
that kind of go beyond that. And of course,

00:10:36.890 --> 00:10:42.889
there's this very strange dynamic where on
the one hand, if it were straight up said

00:10:42.889 --> 00:10:48.769
the idea of the reps is to encourage the prescription
of medications, many people would say that's

00:10:48.769 --> 00:10:53.720
obviously bad. The doctors should simply be
deciding on the merits whether there's a medication

00:10:53.720 --> 00:10:58.060
that's appropriate for an individual or that
it isn't. And then the pharmaceutical companies

00:10:58.060 --> 00:11:04.579
will typically say that's not why the reps
are there. But at the same time, if the presence

00:11:04.579 --> 00:11:11.129
of these reps made no difference, it doesn't
seem like a worthwhile expense for the pharmaceutical

00:11:11.129 --> 00:11:16.480
companies. So there's this kind of weird dynamic
where what are we to interpret to be the point

00:11:16.480 --> 00:11:22.680
of these representatives? And what does what
do we know about the impact on the rates of

00:11:22.680 --> 00:11:27.839
prescriptions of drugs when there are versus
are not pharmaceutical reps?

00:11:27.839 --> 00:11:33.209
Speaker 6: Well, that's an easy question.
We know if a doctor accepts a lunch that is

00:11:33.209 --> 00:11:41.260
valued at $20 single lunch, that will significantly
change his or her prescribing patterns. So

00:11:41.260 --> 00:11:45.630
obviously, the drug companies aren't stupid
enough to engage in this activity because

00:11:45.630 --> 00:11:51.050
it makes them money that makes them more than
it cost them. That the core issue here, it

00:11:51.050 --> 00:11:58.699
makes no sense on the surface, but the core
issue is that doctors in general like to reciprocate

00:11:58.699 --> 00:12:05.740
politeness and kindness and warmth, and they're
not in the habit of being discourteous to

00:12:05.740 --> 00:12:11.310
people who come saying they're trying to help
them. So the drug reps come and they bring

00:12:11.310 --> 00:12:16.740
information. Some of it is reprints from journal
articles, some of it is glossy charts that

00:12:16.740 --> 00:12:22.570
are very easy to interpret. But that information
is delivered because of its marketing value,

00:12:22.570 --> 00:12:27.130
not because of its informational value, because
it's that's the information that's selected

00:12:27.130 --> 00:12:32.319
that will most likely convince the doctor
to prescribe or consider prescribing the drug.

00:12:32.319 --> 00:12:37.310
So we've got a situation where the drug reps
are playing one game. They're taught how to

00:12:37.310 --> 00:12:40.829
shake hands, they're taught how to look in
the eye. They're taught how to keep notes

00:12:40.829 --> 00:12:47.339
about the kids birthdays and their wives anniversaries.
And they ask all those questions and the doctors

00:12:47.339 --> 00:12:51.999
are trying to get the best information they
can as quickly. Lee as they can to best serve

00:12:51.999 --> 00:12:56.720
their patients. And it's not a fair fight
because the drug reps know that their job

00:12:56.720 --> 00:13:01.430
is to increase prescriptions. They get bonus
on that. They get evaluated on it. They have

00:13:01.430 --> 00:13:08.490
minders that go out and watch them perform
their sales spiel and they get graded on their

00:13:08.490 --> 00:13:17.790
sales spiel. So it's a situation that exploits
doctors desire to have good information and

00:13:17.790 --> 00:13:24.170
innate reaction, to be polite, where politeness
is offered to them. The drug reps know how

00:13:24.170 --> 00:13:28.089
to exploit the politeness.
Speaker 1: What's the lowest hanging fruit

00:13:28.089 --> 00:13:33.800
here? If we were to be able to make some changes
to the way the system works, as you talk about

00:13:33.800 --> 00:13:39.589
a lot of the information on which doctors
are making decisions, is information controlled

00:13:39.589 --> 00:13:44.779
by or created in generated funded by the pharmaceutical
companies? All these different things that

00:13:44.779 --> 00:13:49.129
we've talked about, we haven't even touched
on the direct advertising of drugs to people

00:13:49.129 --> 00:13:54.519
via television, which is almost completely
unique to the United States. What's the lowest

00:13:54.519 --> 00:13:57.339
hanging fruit that would give us the most
dramatic improvements?

00:13:57.339 --> 00:14:03.559
Speaker 6: Yeah. So the lowest hanging fruit
is so low that it's a stunning commentary

00:14:03.559 --> 00:14:09.570
on the power that's controlling American medicine.
When a manuscript is submitted to a medical

00:14:09.570 --> 00:14:14.850
journal, usually the company has played a
role in preparing that manuscript. And the

00:14:14.850 --> 00:14:23.809
manuscript contains a very brief summary of
the data from that study to the peer reviewers

00:14:23.809 --> 00:14:29.339
in the medical journal. Editors don't get
the data to see if the manuscript is accurate

00:14:29.339 --> 00:14:35.351
and complete. But the manufacturers produce
what's called a clinical study report. It's

00:14:35.351 --> 00:14:43.100
an official document. It's often two or 3000
pages long of tabulated data that purports

00:14:43.100 --> 00:14:49.540
and usually does present the outcomes that
the researchers said they were going to study.

00:14:49.540 --> 00:14:54.500
So they can't pick and cherry pick their outcomes.
This is a two or 3000 page document that is

00:14:54.500 --> 00:15:01.850
prepared and it's searchable. And the the
drug, the medical journals, they complain

00:15:01.850 --> 00:15:06.550
that they can't do a transparent peer review
because they don't have the resources to go

00:15:06.550 --> 00:15:12.339
through the primary data and get statisticians
to line up the data with the way the drug

00:15:12.339 --> 00:15:16.490
company coded it and all that. And there's
truth to that. But this clinical study report

00:15:16.490 --> 00:15:22.920
exists. It would cost nothing, nothing for
the journals to say. When you submit your

00:15:22.920 --> 00:15:28.750
article, your manuscript, you must submit
a clinical study report if you want to redact

00:15:28.750 --> 00:15:33.430
a little bit about how we make the gel code
or whatever, you can do that if you think

00:15:33.430 --> 00:15:39.639
it's a corporate secret, but you must submit
that. So why don't the journals do this obvious

00:15:39.639 --> 00:15:45.480
thing? And once you learn how to get around
the clinical study reports, as I did in litigation,

00:15:45.480 --> 00:15:50.889
it's pretty quick to figure out what's going
on. Why don't they do it? They don't do it

00:15:50.889 --> 00:15:55.639
because they're in an enormous conflict of
interest position. They make a lot of their

00:15:55.639 --> 00:16:01.439
money from selling reprints back to the drug
companies for the drug reps to hand out to

00:16:01.439 --> 00:16:09.000
docs like your dad. Mm hmm. So if the New
England Journal is going to make $900,000

00:16:09.000 --> 00:16:14.430
selling reprints of the Vioxx article that
misled doctors and had convinced them that

00:16:14.430 --> 00:16:19.650
it was safe to prescribe and didn't increase
the risk of cardiovascular death. The New

00:16:19.650 --> 00:16:27.959
England Journal wants to have that article
published in its pages and sell 900,000 reprints.

00:16:27.959 --> 00:16:34.959
If they had the clinical study report, they
would have known that the article was misleading

00:16:34.959 --> 00:16:40.050
would be two kind of word, but misleadingly
omitted three heart attacks and didn't present

00:16:40.050 --> 00:16:45.449
the data on cardiovascular risk. And the New
England Journal would have understood that

00:16:45.449 --> 00:16:49.560
this was a bad drug. It never should have
been on the market, and it should have been

00:16:49.560 --> 00:16:53.930
withdrawn immediately when the results of
that study came out. But the New England Journal

00:16:53.930 --> 00:16:59.160
won't do it because they're going to miss
the reprint sales and the Lancet and JAMA.

00:16:59.160 --> 00:17:04.510
And it's that's the way it works. They missed
their reprint sales. Their advertising revenues

00:17:04.510 --> 00:17:08.620
will go down. Their impact factor will go
down. They won't be the world recognized as

00:17:08.620 --> 00:17:15.480
the world's greatest journal. So what's happening
is the prestige, much like the prestige of

00:17:15.480 --> 00:17:21.180
H. Brigham in Boston, where they M.G. Brigham,
they're fabulous world class hospitals. They

00:17:21.180 --> 00:17:27.500
spend a fortune on advertising for their branding.
And the journals are in the same business

00:17:27.500 --> 00:17:33.500
of maintaining their branding for their journals.
And that means that they're willing to sacrifice

00:17:33.500 --> 00:17:37.670
the quality of the information that doctors
get when they read their journals in order

00:17:37.670 --> 00:17:43.320
to maintain the branding factor of the journals.
Speaker 1: Are eye opening stuff, much of

00:17:43.320 --> 00:17:48.549
it not the most exciting to read about, but
it is important to know what's happening.

00:17:48.549 --> 00:17:52.460
We've been speaking with. Dr. John Abramson,
who, by the way, we just lost your video,

00:17:52.460 --> 00:17:56.650
but we could hear you fine the entire time
and it couldn't come as a better at a better

00:17:56.650 --> 00:18:00.940
time, since this is the end of the interview.
There you are. You're back. The book is Sickening

00:18:00.940 --> 00:18:04.279
How Big Pharma Broke American Health Care
and How We Can Repair It. We're linking to

00:18:04.279 --> 00:18:06.780
the book and we really appreciate your time
today.

00:18:06.780 --> 00:18:08.640
Speaker 6: Well, thanks very much for having
me

